### Metrics to track

| Metric                   | What it measures                             |
| ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |
| Task completion rate     | Completed tasks divided by planned tasks     |
| Focus hours              | Time recorded in valid focus sessions        |
| Time estimation accuracy | Estimated versus actual task duration        |
| Streak consistency       | Days meeting the user's activity goal        |
| Goal completion          | Progress toward weekly and monthly goals     |
| Category distribution    | How time is divided among activities         |
| Focus-session completion | Sessions completed versus started            |
| Personal records         | Best days, longest streaks, and milestones   |
| Game performance         | Scores, completion times, and personal bests |

### Personal productivity score

Use a transparent, explainable score rather than an opaque AI-generated number.

One possible initial model:

\\[ P = 0.35C + 0.30F + 0.20G + 0.15S \\]

Where:

- \\(C\\) = task completion score.
- \\(F\\) = progress toward the user's focus-time target.
- \\(G\\) = goal progress score.
- \\(S\\) = consistency score.

Each component is normalized to a 0–100 scale, and the total score is capped at 100.

These weights are illustrative and should be user-adjustable or validated during testing. The score should compare users against their own goals rather than rank their worth against other people.

### AI-powered weekly review

A future AI coach can generate a review such as:

> You completed 14 of your 17 planned quests this week. Coding accounted for 6.2 hours of focus time, and your most consistent period was Tuesday through Thursday. Your next improvement opportunity is to estimate larger tasks more accurately.

The system should base these observations on recorded data, clearly distinguish observations from suggestions, and avoid inventing activity or claiming to know what happened outside the app.

